 -- dsh (deepseek harness) <dsh@localhost>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 04:00:00 +0800

snapctrlalt (1.2.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The selection handles now work under every annotation tool, and take
    priority over drawing near the border.  They used to respond only while the
    select tool was active, but releasing a selection switches the tool to
    rectangle, so trying to fine tune an edge drew a rectangle instead.  This
    follows how Flameshot and ksnip behave: the selection stays adjustable at
    any time, and only the middle of the region draws.
  * The handles are always visible now (they used to disappear with any tool
    other than select, so there was nothing to aim at), and the pointer turns
    into the matching resize arrow near an edge or corner.
  * tests/test_coords.py no longer compares two grabs of the live desktop.  It
    cross-checks the overlay export against a direct crop of the same capture,
    so a screen that changes between grabs can no longer fail the suite.

 -- dsh (deepseek harness) <dsh@localhost>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 02:50:00 +0800

snapctrlalt (1.2.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed clicks on the annotation toolbar resetting the selection.  A click
    that landed between two buttons was not recognised as a button, fell
    through to the "clicked outside the selection" branch, and started a new
    selection -- so choosing a tool often threw away the region and forced a
    second capture.  Any click inside the toolbar rectangle is now consumed by
    the toolbar; clicks on empty canvas still start a new selection.
  * Added an explicit "copy to clipboard" button (clipboard icon) to the
    toolbar.  The confirmation tick was the only way to copy and it was easy to
    mistake for "save", which opens a file dialog.
  * Toolbar tooltips and the settings wording now state plainly that finishing
    always copies to the clipboard and that the "after" option only decides
    whether a file is written as well.
  * A configured save directory that no longer exists (for example a config
    copied to another machine) now falls back to the system pictures directory
    and repairs the setting instead of creating a stranger's absolute path.
  * Initialised the toolbar rectangle before the first paint; a click arriving
    before the overlay had drawn could raise AttributeError.

 -- dsh (deepseek harness) <dsh@localhost>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 02:10:00 +0800

snapctrlalt (1.2.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed the settings window never appearing.  It crashed while being built:
    Gtk.Label has no set_border_width(), and the exception happened after the
    window was created but before it was shown, so the process stayed alive
    with no visible dialog.  The window is now built correctly and verified by
    opening it for real.
  * The settings window was also reworked: hotkey rows have a "record" button
    that captures the combination you press, the registered hotkey state is
    shown, invalid hotkeys and identical capture/quit keys are rejected before
    saving, and the interface scale, save format and PRIMARY-clipboard options
    are exposed.
  * --settings now opens the window in its own process instead of being
    forwarded to a resident instance, so it always comes up.
  * "delay" defaults to 3 again, matching the documentation; the spin button
    minimum of 1 used to turn the old default of 0 into 1.

 -- dsh (deepseek harness) <dsh@localhost>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 00:20:00 +0800

snapctrlalt (1.2.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The end-to-end suite now draws a rectangle with real mouse input and checks
    the exported pixels: the stroke has to land on all four expected edges and
    the interior has to stay untouched.  This closes the last gap in the
    "what you select is what you get" guarantee -- the annotation path was
    previously only covered offscreen.

 -- dsh (deepseek harness) <dsh@localhost>  Fri, 25 Sep 2026 23:59:00 +0800

snapctrlalt (1.2.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed the selection bounds on multi-monitor desktops whose virtual screen
    does not start at (0, 0) -- a monitor placed to the left of or above the
    primary one gives the root window a negative origin.  Every clamp used to
    assume 0 as the lower bound, which would have pushed selections on that
    monitor off by the origin; the bounds now come from the calibrated canvas
    rectangle, and "select all" uses it too.
  * Made bin/snapctrlalt robust when it is reached through a symlink: it now
    looks for the package next to both the link and the resolved path, so a
    link into a source checkout and a ~/.local install both work, while a dpkg
    install still uses the system packages without touching sys.path.
  * packaging/debian/rules now explains what to do instead of failing with a
    bare make error when debhelper is not installed.
  * Test suite grew to 36 checks: virtual screen origins that are not (0, 0),
    and 1.25x / 1.75x fractional scaling with a full-screen sweep asserting no
    accumulated rounding error.

 -- dsh (deepseek harness) <dsh@localhost>  Fri, 25 Sep 2026 23:50:00 +0800

snapctrlalt (1.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Linux port of SnapCtrlAlt: global hotkey, full screen overlay, annotation
    tools, clipboard output, tray resident.
  * Fixed the selection offset on HiDPI displays.  The overlay ran under GDK's
    scaling, so the event coordinates, the window allocation and the captured
    image were derived from different scaling factors; on this machine the
    2880x1800 root window even produced a 5760x3600 capture that was GDK's own
    upscale rather than a real resolution.  The overlay now runs at device
    pixel scale, so the event coordinates, the window, the X11 root window and
    the captured pixels are the same space, and a runtime calibration verifies
    the pointer mapping against the X11 root pointer, correcting any residual
    translation or scaling and logging what it found.
  * The capture path now prefers Xlib, which always returns the root window's
    real pixels, instead of GDK's scaled pixbuf.
  * Added independent UI scaling for the toolbar, magnifier and handles, so
    keeping the coordinates exact no longer makes the interface tiny on HiDPI
    panels.
  * Added tests/test_coords.py: calibration maths across 1x, 1.5x, 2x and 3x,
    pointer self-check behaviour including faulty samples, and a pixel exact
    end-to-end check that the selected region equals the exported image.

 -- dsh (deepseek harness) <dsh@localhost>  Fri, 25 Sep 2026 16:00:00 +0800

